   "applicability": "The proposed receipt, clinical review, video consultation, storage and retention occur in Region B, so Region B law is relevant to the Region B recipient. However, the Region B Health Records Act's operative provisions in Sections 4 to 8 apply only to a covered provider or service agent, and Section 9 applies only to a data host. Harrow is not a covered provider because it does not bill a Region B health insurer and is not an insurer. It is not a service agent because it keeps the referral in its own clinical record for its own care, rather than acting on behalf of a covered provider. It is not a data host because it does not store records on behalf of another person. The Records Access Act provisions apply to computing providers, and the facts do not establish that Harrow provides storage, hosting or computing services; the clinical referral portal is described only as the transmission method.",
   "coverage_note": "I reviewed the complete supplied tables of contents and read the Health Records Act definitions, application, permitted uses and disclosures, service-agent agreements, minimum necessary, security, breach notification, data-host retention, access, complaints, penalties and regulations provisions, together with all Records Access Act provisions. The decisive scope distinctions are covered provider, service agent, data host and computing provider. On the stated facts, Harrow is none of these: it is a private clinic receiving and retaining the material for its own care, does not bill an insurer, and stores no records for another person. Thus no Region B transfer gate, clinical-use restriction, portal-security duty, data-host retention rule, access deadline, complaint exposure or Trusted Recipient Scheme condition is engaged. The assessment does not address A1 law governing Kestrel's export or the patient's consent, and it does not assess any unidentified third-party portal operator."
  },
  "region_a": {
