{"answers":[{"question_id":"region_a:q1","answer":"The applicable Region B regime is the Region B Records Access Act, but it does not currently apply to Harrow on the supplied facts. That Act empowers the Records Access Commissioner to order a “computing provider” to produce records stored by or accessible to it, in Region B or elsewhere, only for national-security purposes or the investigation of serious crime. A computing provider is a person providing storage, hosting or computing services in Region B, or a Region B-incorporated provider of those services elsewhere. Harrow is described instead as a specialist health-care provider that keeps the referral in its own clinical record for its own care and does not store records for anyone else; the facts do not establish that it provides computing, hosting or storage services. This is consistent with the already reported finding in `records_access_act_computing_provider_regime`, which is not_applicable.

Accordingly, no current Region B public-authority production or access power under the Records Access Act is engaged against Harrow merely because it receives and retains this referral. No public-authority request or order is presently reported, and no Region B rule supplied prevents the clinics from using Commission-approved transfer clauses or requires supplementary transfer measures for this proposed transfer. The absence of Trusted Recipient Scheme certification does not alter that conclusion under the Region B provisions reviewed.

If Harrow were acting as a computing provider, the position would differ: the Commissioner could issue an order without court authorisation for the statutory purposes, could prohibit disclosure of the order, and the person to whom the records relate would have no right under the Act to notification or review. The Act also does not require the provider to decrypt records or produce keys it does not hold and cannot obtain. Contractual transfer clauses could not prevent compliance with such a statutory order; any resulting conflict would have to be addressed by measures under the other applicable law or by the transfer parties. No such conditional computing-provider status is established here.","facts":[{"fact_id":"f_3707d0e0f8a9","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_febc895bce9e","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_85110fb9a3e5","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_d75ec85c160f","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_1d30f2f75c58","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_a7a94423ed89","path":"","quote":""},{"fact_id":"f_8c44c6e413c5","path":"","quote":""}],"citations":["B-RAA 2","B-RAA 3","B-RAA 4","B-RAA 5","B-RAA 6"],"quote_id":"B-RAA 3#L1","requirement_ids":["records_access_act_computing_provider_regime"]}],"requirements":[]}

